Description: Bounds-check the string-literal buffer to prevent a global buffer overflow
 The lexer copies every character of a Fortran string/character literal into
 the fixed 8192-byte global buffer string_buf with no bounds check
 (lexer.l rule for <str_sq,str_dq>.).  A literal longer than MAX_STRING_LEN,
 including one assembled across continuation lines, writes past the buffer and
 corrupts adjacent globals.  Reproduced with AddressSanitizer:
 global-buffer-overflow write at lexer.l, yylex.
 .
 The buffer only feeds a debug print; string contents never affect the emitted
 dependencies (module/use names are lexed as identifiers), so silently dropping
 the overflow characters is safe and leaves dependency output unchanged.
Author: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1155574
Forwarded: https://bugs.debian.org/1155574
Last-Updated: 2026-11-27
--- a/lexer.l
+++ b/lexer.l
@@ -89,7 +89,8 @@
                     RETURN(UNTERMINATED_STRING);
                 }
 
-<str_sq,str_dq>. { *string_buf_ptr++ = yytext[0]; }
+<str_sq,str_dq>. { if (string_buf_ptr < string_buf + MAX_STRING_LEN - 1)
+                      *string_buf_ptr++ = yytext[0]; }
 
 
 
